# Lesson 4, new exercise: the basic finesse at no-trump. Ace-queen in dummy, lead from your hand toward it. Duck the first spade first, so a losing finesse costs only one down (Mike, 2026-11-25).
title: The ninth trick: a finesse
id: L04-03
type: exercise
lesson: 4
concepts: 4.1
student: S
board: 59
dealer: S
vulnerable: none

deal:            # spades  hearts  diamonds  clubs
  N  962    K84    AQ5    Q842
  E  3      QT652  JT93   T73
  S  A54    A93    862    AKJ5
  W  KQJT87 J7     K74    96

auction:
  S 1NT    = 15-17 points, balanced
     not *: 16 points and a balanced hand: what does the lesson open?
  W Pass
  N 3NT    = 10+ points: game
  E Pass
  S Pass
  W Pass

auction recap:
  You opened 1NT and partner raised to game. You are declarer, because you
  bid no-trump first, and so West will lead to the first trick.

planning:
  winners: 8
  spades: 1 = The ace.
  hearts: 2 = The ace and king.
  diamonds: 1 = The ace. The queen becomes a ninth trick only if West has the king and you lead toward dummy's ace-queen.
  clubs: 4 = Eight clubs with the ace, king, queen and jack: four tricks.
  remind: You need 9 tricks. Count the obvious winners in each suit: spades, hearts, diamonds, clubs.
  remind: Where can the ninth trick come from? Which hand should lead that suit?
  remind: If the ninth trick doesn't come, what could West's spades do to you?
play:
  1   W SK  N S2  E S3  S S4*
      note: Eight obvious winners: a spade, two hearts, a diamond and four clubs. There's no obvious ninth trick. It can come only from the ♦Q, if West holds the king and you finesse. That finesse might lose to East, so get ready for it first.
      S4 key: Duck. You'll have to lose a spade anyway, so lose it now. If the finesse loses later, East may have no spade left to lead back to West.
      S4 not SA: Did you think about ducking? If you win now and the diamond finesse loses to East, East leads a spade back and West runs the rest of the suit: down two. Lose the spade you have to lose now, while East still has a spade to play.
  2   W SQ  N S6  E H2  S SA
      note: East shows out: no spades left. Now win the ace.
  3   S D2  W D4  N DQ*  E D3
      note: Here is the finesse: lead a diamond from your hand toward dummy's ace-queen.
      DQ key: The finesse, just as you planned. West played low, so play the queen. If West has the king, it can't beat your queen, and the queen wins.
      DQ not DA: Remember the plan? The ninth trick comes from finessing the queen. West has played low, so play the queen now: if West has the king, it wins.
  4   N DA  E D9  S D6  W D7
      note: The finesse worked: that's your ninth trick. Cash the ♦A too. From here on, the order you cash your winners doesn't matter.
      ok: C H
  5   N CQ  E C3  S C5  W C6
      note: Now the clubs. Hearts first would be just as good.
      ok: H
  6   N C2  E C7  S CA  W C9
      note: Over to your hand for the rest of the clubs.
  7   S CK  W S7  N C4  E CT
  8   S CJ  W S8  N C8  E DT
      note: The last club.
  9   S H3  W H7  N HK  E H5
      note: Now the hearts.
  10  N H4  E H6  S HA  W HJ
      note: That's nine tricks.
  11  S S5  W SJ  N S9  E HT
      note: Nothing left to win. Give up the rest.
  12  W ST  N D5  E DJ  S D8
  13  W DK  N H8  E HQ  S H9
      note: 9 tricks: 3NT made.

play recap:
  The plan: count eight obvious winners, duck the first spade, then lead a
  diamond toward dummy's ace-queen, hoping West has the king.

  That is the finesse. West had to play before dummy, so when West played
  low, the queen won. If East had held the king, the queen would have lost,
  and you would have gone down. But because you ducked the first spade,
  East would have had no spade to lead back, and you would have been down
  only one. Win the first spade, and East leads a spade back to West's
  winners: down two.
